<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexios III of Trebizond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1338–<a href="/years/1390">1390</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Alexios III Megas Komnenos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexios III Megas Komnenos was emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> from 1349 to 1390. He restored order after years of civil war and kept the small empire safe by diplomacy, marrying his daughters to neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>, including Taceddin. He founded churches and endowed the Dionysiou monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>, and Sumela monastery prospered under his patronage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-of-trebizond.jpg" alt="Alexios III of Trebizond" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Bender235 at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexios3_komnenos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alexios III was born on 5 October 1338, the son of Emperor Basil of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> and his second wife, Irene of Trebizond, and was first named John. After Basil died in 1340, his first wife Irene Palaiologina succeeded him and sent his children to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, where Alexios was raised at the Byzantine court. At eleven he was sent to Trebizond by Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vi-kantakouzenos">John VI Kantakouzenos</a> to replace his deposed great-uncle Michael. He arrived on 22 December 1349, was accepted by the nobility under the megas doux Niketas Scholares, took the name Alexios and was crowned on 21 January 1350.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The young emperor was at first overshadowed by squabbling aristocrats and withdrew to the coastal castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripolis</a>. With his mother and loyal courtiers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-panaretos">Michael Panaretos</a>, whose chronicle is the main source for the empire's politics, he subdued the nobles one by one. Niketas fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giresun">Kerasous</a> in 1354 and sailed against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trebizond</a> in 1355 but turned back; Alexios took Kerasous and Kenchrina and brought Niketas to Trebizond, where he died in 1360. In 1363 the Kabasites and other nobles tried to assassinate Alexios. He escaped, arrested them and deposed the Metropolitan Niphon for his part in the plot.</p><p>On the frontier Alexios was less successful and suffered a major defeat against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> in 1355. He relied on marriage alliances with neighbouring Muslim rulers: his sister Maria married Fahreddin Kutlubeg of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> in 1352, and four of his daughters married Muslim princes, one of them Tajeddin, emir of Limnia, in 1379. Another daughter, Anna Megale Komnene, became the second wife of King Bagrat V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>. He failed to displace the Genoese and Venetians from the empire's commerce, and in 1373 the Venetians conspired with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrotitsa">Dobrotitsa of Dobruja</a> to put Michael Palaiologos on the throne, a plan abandoned after five days.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alexios III died on 20 March 1390 and was succeeded by his son Manuel III. He had repaired the capital and endowed several monasteries, especially Soumela, and founded the Dionysiou monastery on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a>. Anthony Bryer links this generosity to confiscations after the defeat of the nobles who opposed strong central government: as their estates reverted to the crown he could refound St. Phokas in Kordyle (1362), Soumela (1364), Dionysiou (1374) and Theoskepastos (1376). Alexios also granted charters confirming noble families in their lands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexios III of Trebizond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A chronicle by Michael Panaretos, an official of <b>Alexios III</b>, preserves the history of the fourteenth-century court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Its relations with the Empire of Trebizond mixed raiding and alliance: Taceddin married a daughter of <b>Alexios III</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/taceddinids">Taceddinids</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1371" aria-label="Trabzon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5522 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexios III of Trebizond"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5972 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5972 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,39.72&amp;year=1371">What was here in 1371? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
